Vectordb companies be like. In general, this kills the 'performance moat' for every vertical infrastructure play. Precision and recall, database tuning, kernel optimizations, and inference speed are all becoming weekend projects for AI agents. If autoresearch can match your engineering team's output, you don't have a moat; you have a feature.

The remaining moats are what requires taste over search, which is more likely to lead to architectural breakthroughs.

@Zai_org: "Vector-DB-Bench: 6x Performance Boost. In high-performance database optimization, GLM-5.1 reached 21.5k QPS over 600+ iterations and 6,000+ tool calls. This is 6x the performance of a standard 50-turn session."

With the release of Qwen3.6-27B, the gap between dense and mixture-of-experts (MoE) models is shrinking, which is beneficial for local AI since MoE models like 35B-A3B are more compatible with low-budget GPUs and support much longer contexts (256k full easily on 24GB VRAM).

In a same-scale comparison (27B dense vs 35B-A3B MoE), dense models still outperform in most tasks, but the gap has narrowed in 7 out of 10 benchmarks, particularly in coding tasks where the SWE-bench Multilingual gap decreased from +9.0 to +4.1. The exception is Terminal-Bench 2.0, where dense models significantly outperformed MoE models, increasing from +1.1 to +7.8.

@Alibaba_Qwen: "🚀 Meet Qwen3.6-27B, our latest dense, open-source model, packing flagship-level coding power! Yes, 27B, and Qwen3.6-27B punches way above its weight. What's new: Outstanding agentic coding — surpasses Qwen3.5-397B-A17B across all major coding benchmarks, strong reasoning across text & multimodal tasks, supports thinking & non-thinking modes, fully open under Apache 2.0. Smaller model. Bigger results. Community's favorite. We can't wait to see what you build with Qwen3.6-27B!"

